[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.CI_PR
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[CI_PR]
overview: How AI agents deliver and verify code — through pull requests whose CI is the single source of truth
purpose: Keep verification reproducible and cheap: CI runs the exact configurations on every push, so agents spend no time or tokens on local builds and never report "works on my machine"
applies_to: Every code change made by an agent (author agents, orchestrators, reviewers)

[CORE_PRINCIPLE]
A change is verified by CI on its pull request, not by the agent's local environment.
Agents do NOT configure, build, run tests or launch the app locally when CI covers that check.
CI runs are free: push as often as needed, run many PRs in parallel.

[FLOW]
1. Branch: one branch per task from the current integration branch, named `wip/<issue>-<slug>`
2. Code: write the change; reference the issue in commits (`refs #N` / `closes #N`)
3. Push + PR: push the branch and open a DRAFT pull request into the integration branch — CI starts on its own
4. Red CI: download the failing job's log, find the root cause, fix, push again (CI re-runs on push)
5. Green CI: every check finished and passed — partial green is not green
6. Review: an independent reviewer (not the author) reads the diff; findings are fixed by the author on the same branch
7. Merge: the orchestrator (or the owner) marks the PR ready and squash-merges it; the author agent never merges
8. Close: close the issue if the merge did not, unsubscribe from the PR

[RULES]
- No local builds by agents: no configure, build, test run, headless/screenshot run — unless the owner asks or no CI job covers the check
- CI is the evidence: an agent reports PR URL, head SHA and the CI result (all checks), quoting log lines for gates such as screenshot diffs
- Parallelism is limited only by file overlap, not by CI capacity — independent tasks go out at the same time
- Before review: fetch the integration branch; if it moved or files overlap with another open PR, MERGE it into the task branch (merge commit, never rebase)
- Fix by root cause: never skip, disable or quarantine a test; never push an empty commit or close/reopen a PR to re-trigger CI
- Re-run a CI job only for an infrastructure failure (runner lost, checkout/install died) — at most once
- History: never force-push, never rewrite someone else's branch, never delete branches from an agent session
- Direct commits to the integration branch: documentation only (handoff, design docs); all code goes through a PR
- Wait for CI to finish: an author agent does not stop while checks are still running

[WRITING_CODE_WITHOUT_A_LOCAL_COMPILER]
Since CI is the first compiler, write code that survives every configuration on the first push:
- Include what you use — do not rely on a precompiled header or transitive includes
- Keep string literals portable across compilers' source/execution charsets (spell non-ASCII bytes explicitly)
- Keep line endings of each touched file as they are
- Read the CI matrix once (workflow files) to know which compilers, sanitizers and modes run

[PROJECT_NOTES]
This project:
- Integration branch and current wave: see `docs/agent/handoff/PHASE0-STATUS.md`
- CI (#830): `.github/workflows/build.yml` on PRs and pushes, `.github/workflows/nightly.yml` once a night on aibranch; every build but Linux Clang Debug (the one static-engine build, #223) uses the shared engine
- Every PR event, drafts included: the 6 fast checks — Coverage (Linux GCC Debug), Linux Clang Debug, Windows MSVC Debug, Linux ASan+UBSan, Linux GCC no Assimp (SNK_WITH_ASSIMP=OFF), Editor visual smoke — after the `Plan` job, which decides what runs. A PR that changes only `docs/**` and `*.md` runs Plan and CI summary alone (no paths-ignore on pull_request: a run that never starts would leave required checks waiting)
- The Coverage job is blocking (it is the only GCC Debug unit-test run and carries the GL-only and desc byte-stability gates); only its report generation and upload steps are non-blocking
- Marking the PR ready (`ready_for_review`) does not re-run the fast checks when all six already passed in one earlier Build run of this PR on the same head SHA and on the base SHA aibranch has now (the earlier run's base is the name of its `Base <sha>` job: the first parent of the merge commit the run built, #836; they show as skipped and stay green); a new push or a base that moved runs them again
- A non-draft PR that touches any header (`.h`/`.hpp`/`.inl`, `SNK_API` headers live outside `include/` too), `CMakeLists.txt`, `*.cmake`/`*.cmake.in`, `cmake/**`, `CMakePresets.json` or `.github/**` also runs Windows MSVC Shared+PCH and Linux GCC Shared (the engine as a shared library: exports, ABI); so does a PR with 3000+ files and every push to aibranch/main. Behaviour change (#830): drafts and other PRs no longer build Linux GCC Shared
- Required checks (branch protection, set by the owner): `Plan`, `CI summary` and the 6 fast jobs. A job skipped by `if` counts as passed, so a failed `Plan` would leave everything green: `CI summary` (`if: always()`, last job) fails when `Plan` did not succeed or a job the plan asked for did not pass; skipped is accepted only where the plan skipped it
- Nightly (`nightly.yml`, 23:37 UTC, or Actions > Nightly > Run workflow): Linux TSan, SDK package (Linux), SDK package (Linux, no Assimp), Windows package (Release, Debug, check), Extract benchmark (its build must pass, the measurement is report-only); a night with no new commit since the last green one runs nothing
- Default branch caveat: GitHub fires `schedule` only from the workflow file on the default branch (main), and `uses: ./.github/workflows/...` is read from the branch the run started on. Until the owner decides how nightly reaches main, it runs only by hand (Actions > Nightly > Run workflow, branch aibranch)
- Red night (a failed nightly plan counts as red): the `Report (nightly)` job opens the issue "Nightly CI red" (labels bug, ci, P0) with the run and the commits since the last green night, or comments on the open one; the next green night closes it. A red night comes before new work: find the commit, fix it in a PR with `closes #<issue>`
- Merge after a green run on the non-draft head: green draft checks plus whatever ready_for_review added
- Screenshot gate: the visual smoke job fails when a reference diff exceeds the limit (`tools/headless/check_smoke.sh`); reference PNGs change only with the owner's decision
- MSVC builds without PCH and without `/utf-8`
- Since #223 the fast tier builds the shared engine (Windows MSVC Debug, Coverage, ASan, ...): a free function or class in a public engine header that tests or the editor call needs `SNK_API`, otherwise the shared link fails; Linux Clang Debug is the static build
